SilverLining for Linux and MacOS: Getting Started

This SDK includes complete source code; you'll need to build the 
SilverLining libraries for your system using it.

BUILDING FOR OPENGL:

First, ensure you have the necessary dependencies installed:

* GL
* GLU
* glut or freeglut (needed for the sample application)

Next, just run:

make

from the top level of the SilverLining-SDK-FullSource directory you extracted.
This will build SilverLining's libraries into the lib/linux or lib/macos
directory, depending on your system.

To make sure everything worked, cd into the SampleCode/OpenGLExample directory,
and run make there as well. Run the resulting OpenGLExample binary, and a
procedural sky with clouds should appear. If instead you see a bunch of GL
related errors, try updating your graphics drivers.

BUILDING FOR VULKAN:

First, ensure you have the Vulkan SDK installed, as well
as recent video drivers. See https://vulkan.lunarg.com/sdk/home#linux

Next, just run:

make vulkan

from the top level of the SilverLining-SDK-FullSource directory you extracted.
This will build SilverLining's libraries into the lib/linux directory.

To make sure everything worked, cd into the SampleCode/VulkanExample directory,
and run make there as well. You may need to install the GLFW3, Xxf86vm, Xrandr,
and/or Xi development packages for this to build successfully. (On Ubuntu,
this would be done with 
sudo apt install libglfw3-dev libxxf86vm-dev libxi-dev libxrandr-dev 
for example)

